    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The pre-Christian religion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> worshipped gods such as Odin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thor">Thor</a>, Freyr and Freyja, with sacrifices, feasts and rituals led by chieftains and kings, in a world of nine realms around the world tree Yggdrasil.</li><li>Its myths, of a world made from the body of a giant and destroyed at Ragnarök, are known mainly from Icelandic texts written down by Christians in the 13th century, two centuries after the conversion.</li><li>Displaced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> between the 10th and 12th centuries, it gave English four of its weekday names and a mythology that has inspired art and literature from Wagner to Tolkien and modern film.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>A soapstone mould found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, from the 10th century, was made to cast both Christian crosses and the hammers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thor">Thor</a>, so that a smith could sell to customers of either faith.</p></aside>
    </section>

